[size=4][b]Description[/b][/size]
[size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Not Enough Waypoints[/b] adds six waypoints to Baldur's Gate 3, in the places you keep walking back to.

The game puts most of its waypoints at the edge of a place: outside the Emerald Grove, outside the goblin camp's temple, outside Moonrise Towers. The traders, quest givers and doors you need are often a long walk further in. These six waypoints stand where that walk is longest.

Each one copies the game's own Blighted Village waypoint (the invisible shrine, the boulder in a colour that suits the place, and the rune projected onto its face), and works like the game's own: walk near one to unlock it, then travel to it from the map or any other waypoint.[/color][/size]
[list]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]The Hollow[/b] (Act 1): on the floor of the Hollow in the Emerald Grove, against the ledge below Okta's and Auntie Ethel's stalls, across from Dammon's forge.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Underdark - Arcane Tower[/b] (Act 1): on the landing outside the tower's front door, backed against the cliff edge to the east.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Shattered Sanctum[/b] (Act 1): inside the goblin camp's temple, backed into the northwest corner of the central hall south of Dror Ragzlin's doors.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Moonrise Towers - Main Floor[/b] (Act 2): on the main floor, on the west edge of the central entrance hall beside the bazaar.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Open Hand Temple[/b] (Act 3): in the street in front of the temple in Rivington, where the refugees beg.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Guildhall[/b] (Act 3): in the Guildhall's lower main hall, backed into the alcove on the south side.[/color][/size][/*]
[/list][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]Every waypoint was placed with the game's own navigation data and checked against everything the game puts nearby, its sub-levels and how far each object reaches included. All six have been seen and checked in game on Patch 8 Hotfix 10. If one still clips into something or stands in the way, please report it on the Bugs tab or on [url=https://github.com/Lumpish-Haggard/NotEnoughWaypoints/issues/new/choose]GitHub[/url], with a screenshot.

[b]New in 1.3:[/b] the arrival points are set up the way the game sets up its own: turned to face their waypoint, with the camera's direction on arrival. They have not been seen in game yet: if arriving at one looks odd, please report it.

[b]New in 1.2.1:[/b] fixes the four waypoints whose runes appeared smeared in 1.1 and 1.2 (Underdark - Arcane Tower, Shattered Sanctum, Moonrise Towers - Main Floor and Guildhall). All four now sit on boulders at clean new spots, and all six waypoints have been seen and checked in game on Patch 8 Hotfix 10. Existing saves update automatically when you load each level, with no new game needed. Moving indoor runes back onto prop-built walls is planned for a later update.

[b]New in 1.2:[/b] the mod is safe to remove. [b]Not Enough Waypoints Uninstaller[/b], under Optional files, takes the waypoints out of a save, so that you can remove the mod in the middle of a playthrough. See [i]Removing the mod[/i] below. It was designed from a save made with the mod and checked against the game's own story, but has not been tried in game yet: if it leaves anything behind, please report it.

Open source (MIT): [url=https://github.com/Lumpish-Haggard/NotEnoughWaypoints]github.com/Lumpish-Haggard/NotEnoughWaypoints[/url][/color][/size]

[size=4][b]Installation instructions[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]Install this mod with your mod manager:[/color]
[/size]
[list]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]BG3 Mod Manager[/b]: import the zip, move [i]Not Enough Waypoints[/i] to the active list, then save and export the load order.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Vortex[/b]: install, enable, deploy.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Manual[/b]: put [i]NotEnoughWaypoints.pak[/i] in [i]%LOCALAPPDATA%\Larian Studios\Baldur's Gate 3\Mods[/i] and enable it in your mod manager.[/color][/size][/*]
[/list][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]Load a save and walk to one of the places above.[/color][/size][/*]
[/list][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]It works in a new game and in an existing save. When an update moves a waypoint, it is moved in your save too.[/color][/size]

[size=4][b]Removing the mod[/b][/size]
[size=3][color=#D4D4D8]A save that has used the mod keeps its waypoints. Remove only the mod and a waypoint you unlocked stays in the travel list, without its name, and leads nowhere. Take the waypoints out first, with the uninstaller from Optional files:[/color][/size]
[list=1]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]Install [i]Not Enough Waypoints Uninstaller[/i] like the mod.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]Turn [b]Not Enough Waypoints off[/b] and [b]the uninstaller on[/b] (BG3 Mod Manager: export the load order).[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]Load your save. The game says that Not Enough Waypoints is missing: carry on. Its waypoints are gone from the travel list.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Save.[/b][/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]Turn the uninstaller off. The next time you load that save, the game says once that the uninstaller is missing: carry on, and save again.[/color][/size][/*]
[/list][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]From that save on, the mod's waypoints are out of the playthrough. The uninstaller locks each waypoint the way the story locks a waypoint when its act ends, then takes it out of the story. It does nothing while the mod itself is loaded, so loading both by mistake breaks nothing. It also cleans up a save that the mod was removed from without it.[/color][/size]

[size=4][b]Main features[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Six new waypoints[/b] across all three acts, where the nearest one is a long walk away.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Built like the game's own[/b]: each waypoint is a copy of the Blighted Village boulder and rune, in a colour that suits the place, and unlocks the same way.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]They follow the story[/b]: each is locked with the rest of its act when the story moves on, like the game's own. The Hollow is also locked when the druids seal the Emerald Grove, together with the game's Grove waypoint, so it never gets you past the barrier.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Sovereign Glut comes along[/b]: the Arcane Tower counts as an Underdark waypoint, so he follows the party there.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Placed with the game's own data[/b]: every boulder rests on free floor from the game's navigation grid, clear of everything the game puts there, with room for the whole party in front. Nothing is a guessed coordinate.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Safe to remove[/b]: the uninstaller takes the waypoints out of a save before you remove the mod.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Nothing replaced[/b]: the mod adds its own objects, one small story script and the waypoint names. It replaces none of the game's files.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8][b]Multiplayer[/b]: waypoints unlock for the whole party, as usual.[/color][/size][/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]Baldur's Gate 3 Patch 8. The waypoints are placed and checked against the Patch 8 Hotfix 10 game files.[/color][/size][/*]
[*][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]No other mods.[/color][/size][/*]
[/list][size=3][color=#D4D4D8]The waypoint names are in English only for now.[/color][/size]

